    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can u also explain the chemistry of why that happens? like why does the solubility change so much as it gets more alkaline? i need to explain the 'why' part in the report so the grower understands the mechanism.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The decreased solubility as $\\text{pH}$ rises is primarily due to the formation of less soluble hydroxide complexes. Specifically, as $\\text{pH}$ increases, the concentration of hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) rises, causing metallic ions like $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$ to react with these ions: $\\text{M}^{2+} + 2\\text{OH}^- \\rightarrow \\text{M}(\\text{OH})_2$, where $\\text{M}$ is the metal. These resulting metal hydroxides have very low solubility in neutral or alkaline conditions, effectively precipitating out of the soil solution and becoming unavailable to the grapevine roots.",
      "source": "target",
